<figure class="lms-figure" aria-labelledby="fig-tip">
  <figcaption id="fig-tip" class="lms-figure-caption">A $40 sale with an $8 tip</figcaption>
  <ol class="lms-timeline">
    <li class="lms-timeline-item"><span class="lms-timeline-when">1:15 PM</span><span class="lms-timeline-what"><strong>Sale: $40.00 authorized.</strong> The customer taps; the bank holds $40 (or $40 plus a tip allowance, depending on the terminal setup).</span></li>
    <li class="lms-timeline-item"><span class="lms-timeline-when">1:16 PM</span><span class="lms-timeline-what"><strong>Tip: $8.00.</strong> On the P18 the customer chooses the tip on the screen, or the cashier adjusts it from the signed slip. Either way the sale is now $48.00.</span></li>
    <li class="lms-timeline-item lms-timeline-item-mark"><span class="lms-timeline-when">Before batch close</span><span class="lms-timeline-what"><strong>Last chance to adjust.</strong> Tip adjustments only work while the batch is open. After it closes, the amount is final.</span></li>
    <li class="lms-timeline-item"><span class="lms-timeline-when">End of day</span><span class="lms-timeline-what"><strong>Batch closes with $48.00.</strong> That is what settles and what the customer sees on their statement.</span></li>
    <li class="lms-timeline-item"><span class="lms-timeline-when">Payday</span><span class="lms-timeline-what"><strong>Tip reaches the employee</strong> through the tip report and payroll. The card fee on the tip is a business cost, not the employee's.</span></li>
  </ol>
</figure>
